{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"sound-bumpkinly-thrush","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## The defect\n\nTwo test sites outside the live-DC tier are the same **fail-OPEN** class as bug\n`0e1d-c698-c38d-4c3e` / `f0fb-de7a-b315-4508`, under a different phrasing. Each spawns a REAL\nchild with `subprocess.run`, never asserts a returncode, and its ONLY assertion is that some\nstring is ABSENT from the captured output. A child killed by a signal writes nothing, so the\nabsent-string assertion is trivially satisfied and the test reports GREEN for a run that never\nexecuted.\n\nProven mechanism (bug `0e1d-c698-c38d-4c3e`, 4/4 deterministic): a child that SIGKILLs itself\nyields `returncode == -9` with `stdout == stderr == \"\"`.\n\n### Site 1 — `tests/unit/test_git_floor_gate_tree_skew.py:127`\n\n`test_absent_floor_file_emits_no_workflow_error`. `_run_gate` (line ~54) is\n`subprocess.run([\"bash\", \"-c\", gate_script], capture_output=True)` with no `check=True` and no\nreturncode assertion. The whole test body is three lines and its only assertion is:\n\n    assert \"::error::\" not in (result.stdout + result.stderr)\n\nA reaped or OOM-killed bash child gives empty streams, so the gate script never ran and the test\nstill passes. This is a **success/health oracle** — the dangerous direction.\n\n### Site 2 — `tests/interfaces/facades/test_cli.py:151`\n\n`test_reconcile_is_routed_...` (the non-integration one). Real child via `_cli(...)`. No\nreturncode assertion anywhere in the test; the sole assertion is:\n\n    assert \"unknown subcommand 'reconcile'\" not in (cp.stdout + cp.stderr).lower()\n\nEmpty output from a signal-killed child satisfies it, so a never-started CLI reads as \"the\ndispatcher routed correctly\".\n\n### Borderline (lower risk, worth a look)\n\n`tests/unit/workflow/test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py:243` — `_run_redispatch` asserts\n`completed.returncode != 0`, which a `-9` / `137` exit ALSO satisfies, so the returncode check\ndoes not fully close the gap. It is a message-quality oracle on a deliberately-failing run, so\nthe residual risk is low, but `!= 0` is the wrong shape for the same reason.\n\n## Why it was not fixed in scope\n\nFiled rather than fixed because both files sit outside the file lane of\n`0e1d-c698-c38d-4c3e` (the live-DC test files plus their shared helper) and were held by other\nsessions at the time. They are also a different construct — an absent-string oracle over\n`stdout + stderr`, not the `assert \"Traceback\" not in cp.stderr` shape — so folding them in\nwould have widened that change past what one review can carry.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] Neither site can pass on a signal-killed child: the returncode is asserted, not only the\n      absence of a string in the captured output.\n- [ ] A RED test proves it for each site — the child forced to die on a signal (empty output)\n      makes the assertion fail before the fix, and the failure names the signal after it.\n- [ ] `test_reconcile_workflow_lint.py:243`'s `returncode != 0` is tightened or explicitly\n      justified, since a signal kill satisfies `!= 0`.\n- [ ] Reuse `tests/_child_diag.py` (`child_failure_detail` / `assert_child_ran_clean`) rather\n      than re-deriving the message, so every tier fails identically.","id":"1241-b83c-f8c7-40bf","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Absent-string child oracles fail OPEN on a signal-killed child"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787301393015691001,"uuid":"c17d1047-efea-4c0f-8f93-9355f9bc8ff2"}